#!/usr/bin/env perl
# fillna, ffill, bfill, dropna, drop_duplicates and value_counts, against the
# cases in R's and pandas' own test suites.
#
# Provenance (every table below is frozen output of the generators committed
# beside this file; this test never runs R or Python):
#   pandas 3.0.4 (NumPy 2.4.6), pandas/tests/.  t/frame.cleaning.R.pandas.py
#         prints the %FR, @DD, @FILL, @DROP, %DN, @ROWS and @VC tables; re-run
#             python3 -I t/frame.cleaning.R.pandas.py
#         Each case carries the name of its pandas test, and the .py header
#         lists the files:  frame/methods/test_drop_duplicates.py,
#         test_dropna.py, test_drop.py, test_fillna.py,
#         test_value_counts.py; series/methods/test_fillna.py and
#         test_value_counts.py; base/test_value_counts.py.  Upstream issues
#         carried by those tests: GH 11376, GH 11864 (drop_duplicates), GH
#         19730 (misspelled subset), GH 20516 (empty frames), GH 8303 (dropna
#         on a missing column), GH 41021 (dropna subset), GH 40989 and GH
#         47188 (fillna).  Inputs that upstream draw from
#         np.random.default_rng(2) are replaced by dyadic literals, because
#         only the NA pattern matters to a fill or a drop.
#   R     R 4.6.1 (/home/con/Scripts/r-source), run under R 4.6.2.
#         t/frame.cleaning.R.pandas.R prints the %R table; re-run it with
#             Rscript t/frame.cleaning.R.pandas.R
#         Cases: src/library/base/man/duplicated.Rd \examples (x, warpbreaks,
#         fromLast), tests/reg-tests-1a.R:264 (iris), tests/reg-tests-1d.R:2130
#         (PR#17485, d <- data.frame(f=gl(3,5), i=1:3)) and :1602 (PR#17369,
#         0.3 + 0.6 versus 0.9); src/library/stats/man/na.fail.Rd and
#         complete.cases.Rd \examples (na.omit(DF), airquality) for dropna;
#         src/library/base/man/table.Rd \examples for value_counts.
#
# Shapes.  Every frame is stored as a HoA with its column order and rebuilt as
# each shape the function takes: AoA (columns by position), AoH, HoA and HoH
# (row keys r000, r001, ... so that sorted-key order is row order).  A pandas
# NaN or None, and an R NA, is undef.  Row indices in the tables are 0-based.
#
# Tolerances: none.  Every comparison is exact (is_deeply on stringified
# cells), because a fill or a drop copies cells and never computes.
#
# Deliberate differences, kept so that changing one is a decision:
#   - fillna has no `limit` (pandas fillna(value, limit=n), test_fillna_limit_
#     and_value, series test_fillna_limit); ffill/bfill do, and fillna with
#     limit is not tested.
#   - No axis=1 (test_fillna_with_columns_and_limit, test_single_block_df_with_
#     horizontal_axis, dropna(axis=1)); no thresh=; no limit_area (the
#     test_ffill_bfill_limit_area data is run with `limit` alone, and the
#     expectation recomputed by pandas without limit_area).
#   - fillna/ffill/bfill and dropna treat only undef as missing; pandas and R
#     also treat NaN (README: "Replace NA (undef) cells").  A NaN input is
#     therefore not mapped.
#   - dropna wants `cols` (or `rows`) and an arrayref; pandas defaults to every
#     column and takes a scalar subset ("C").  The scalar form is tested to
#     die, as the README promises.
#   - value_counts always skips undef (pandas dropna=True); dropna=False has no
#     equivalent.  It returns a hash, so pandas' ordering by count has no
#     counterpart, and keys are stringified (a bool is "True" only if it was
#     passed as that string).  DataFrame.value_counts over several columns,
#     normalize=, bins= and the categorical / datetime / period / complex
#     dtype cases have no mapping.
#   - drop_duplicates compares stringified cells (README), so R's 0.3 + 0.6
#     versus 0.9 (reg-tests-1d.R, PR#17369), two doubles that print alike at
#     15 digits, is a documented difference; it is asserted as such below.
#   - HoH is refused by drop_duplicates (README), pandas has no analogue.

require 5.010;
